<% const timing = tokenTiming(token); %>
<div class="card">
  <h2><span class="step">Step 2</span>Access token</h2>
  <p class="hint">The authorization code was exchanged server-side for these tokens.</p>

  <dl class="kv">
    <dt>Granted scope</dt>
    <dd>
      <code><%= token.scope || "not reported" %></code>
      <% if (!luca.grants(token.scope, luca.SCOPES.write)) { %>
        <span class="hint" style="display: block; margin: 4px 0 0;">Read only — mutations will be refused.</span>
      <% } %>
    </dd>
    <dt>Token type</dt>
    <dd><code><%= token.token_type %></code></dd>
    <dt>Issued via</dt>
    <dd><code><%= token.grant_type %></code> at <%= formatTime(timing.obtainedAt) %></dd>
    <dt>Expires</dt>
    <dd>
      <%= formatTime(timing.expiresAt) %>
      (<%= token.expires_in %>s after issue<%= timing.expired ? " — expired" : "" %>)
    </dd>
    <dt>Refreshes</dt>
    <dd><%= token.refresh_count %> since connecting</dd>
  </dl>

  <p class="hint" style="margin: 18px 0 6px;">
    Access token (opaque) — there is nothing in here to decode, and that is the point: an opaque
    token can be revoked the instant a user withdraws access, and tells a client nothing it has no
    business reading. Ask the introspection endpoint if you need to know what one carries.
  </p>
  <pre><%= token.access_token %></pre>
  <p class="hint" style="margin: 0 0 6px;">
    Refresh token — single use. Store the new one after every refresh; presenting a spent one is
    treated as theft and revokes the whole authorization.
  </p>
  <pre><%= token.refresh_token %></pre>

  <form method="post" action="/oauth/disconnect">
    <div class="actions">
      <button class="btn danger" type="submit">Revoke and disconnect</button>
      <span class="hint" style="margin: 0;">
        Calls <code>POST <%= metadata.revocationEndpoint %></code> first, then drops the local copy.
      </span>
    </div>
  </form>
</div>
